  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 243 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern over the CONUS features split flow \nacross the Plains with a deep cut off low over the Mid-Atlantic. \nIndiana is within a broad area of subsidence due to upper-level \nridging and surface high pressure. A trough is moving ashore over \nCalifornia as of this writing, and may become an important feature \nnext week.\n\nTODAY THROUGH TUESDAY\n\nThe weather for the next several days is not anticipated to be that \ndifferent on a day to day basis. Surface high pressure is in control \nand will provide clear skies and light northerly winds. With ideal \nradiative heating/cooling conditions, a large diurnal spread in \ntemperatures is likely. Low temperatures in the 40s to near 50 will \nrapidly rebound into the 70s each afternoon.\n\nConditions also favor nocturnal fog development. Fog should be \npatchy in nature, confined mainly to rural areas and fog-prone \nlocations along rivers. Locally dense fog is possible at times.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY\n\nBy about Wednesday a change in the overall weather pattern appears \nlikely. Surface high pressure slides eastward allowing winds to \nbecome southwesterly. Warm air advection takes hold potentially \nbringing temperatures back into the 80s.\n\nTo our west, ensemble guidance shows the California trough, now cut \noff from the larger scale flow, ejecting into the Plains. \nSimultaneously, Hurricane Polo lifts north and becomes absorbed by \nthe upper low...contributing its moisture to the larger system.\n\nGuidance is a bit mixed on what happens next, but trends have been \ntowards increasing precipitation chances in the Wednesday night \nthrough Friday timeframe. The emerging system should cause a \nbaroclinic zone over the Plains to tighten, which will focus \nprecipitation potential in a SW to NE band. Unfortunately, guidance \ncan do poorly with cut off upper-level lows and is sometimes too \nfast bringing them eastward. Should the same model bias occur, a \nslower arrival time and delayed precip chances could be seen in \nlater forecast updates.\n\nSATURDAY ONWARD\n\nDetails become murky at this point but global models tend to keep \nthe baroclinic zone in the region through the weekend. As such, we \nwill retain at least chance PoPs through Sunday. Thereafter, \nguidance shows a more progressive flow pattern taking hold which \nallows things to begin moving eastward. A return to dry weather is \npossible early next week.",
